From: Abdelhafid DJIDEL <266947999+hafid-logic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 10:40:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests(process): pin timer one-shot retirement, periodic
 catch-up, and deadline edge

### What
Pins `TimerTable::update`: a one-shot timer (interval 0) expires exactly once and then stays idle; a periodic timer catches up by `1 + elapsed/interval` expirations; and a timer fires at its deadline, not one tick before.

### Why it matters
timerfd semantics require exact expiration accounting. The existing suite arms timers but never pins the one-shot count or the strict deadline comparison, so over-counting or an off-by-one boundary passes it.

### Mutation evidence (fail-then-pass)
| Mutant | Existing suite | New test |
|---|---|---|
| one-shot `saturating_add(1)` -> `(2)` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |
| `now_ns < deadline` -> `<=` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |

Spec-Ref: timerfd_create(2) / POSIX timer expiration accounting.

Baseline (clean tree + new test): PASS. Reproduce: `cargo test -p ginux-process --lib timer_update_one_shot`.

Prepared with AI assistance; reviewed by me.
---
 crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

diff --git a/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs b/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
index 3cf50f78..209af037 100644
--- a/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
@@ -2254,4 +2254,31 @@ mod tests {
         poll.remove(5).unwrap();
         poll.add(5, EVENT_WRITE).unwrap();
     }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn timer_update_one_shot_stops_and_periodic_catches_up_exactly() {
+        let mut timers: TimerTable<4> = TimerTable::new();
+        // one-shot (interval 0): a single expiration, then permanently idle
+        let one = timers.create().unwrap();
+        timers.arm(one, 0, 10, 0).unwrap();
+        timers.update(10);
+        assert_eq!(timers.read(one), Ok(1));
+        timers.update(1_000);
+        assert_eq!(timers.read(one), Err(ProcessError::WouldBlock));
+        // periodic: elapsed 100 with deadline 10 / interval 25 => 1 + 3 = 4
+        let per = timers.create().unwrap();
+        timers.arm(per, 0, 10, 25).unwrap();
+        timers.update(100);
+        assert_eq!(timers.read(per), Ok(4));
+        // no further time passed: nothing new
+        timers.update(100);
+        assert_eq!(timers.read(per), Err(ProcessError::WouldBlock));
+        // before the deadline nothing fires
+        let early = timers.create().unwrap();
+        timers.arm(early, 100, 50, 0).unwrap();
+        timers.update(149);
+        assert_eq!(timers.read(early), Err(ProcessError::WouldBlock));
+        timers.update(150);
+        assert_eq!(timers.read(early), Ok(1));
+    }
 }
